#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# series

#% text
# `series(e, x, x0, n)` gives the power series of `e` in `x` about the point `x0`, up to the power n of (x - x0).

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is a truncated sum followed by an order term `O((x - x0)^(n + 1))` for the terms left out.

#% item
# A pole at `x0` gives negative powers (a Laurent series).

#% item
# The coefficients may hold symbols.

#% item
# `normal` turns a series into its truncated sum, an ordinary expression.

#% item
# An order term in the input, `e + O(x^k)`, limits the result to that order.

#% item
# At a branch point the result has fractional powers (a Puiseux series); when the function is real only on the left of the point, the powers are of `x0 - x`, unless `assumptions:` put `x` on one side.

#% item
# `series(e, x -> x0)` gives the leading term and the order of the next one; a list `e` is expanded elementwise.

#% item
# At `x0 = inf` the series is in powers of `1/x`: `n` keeps every term whose power of `1/x` is at most `n`, fractional powers included (`x^(-9/2)` is kept for `n = 5`), and the order term is the next power present. An expansion with factors such as `exp(x)` or `cos(x)` (Kelvin, Struve, Airy functions) keeps the same terms in the series that multiplies each factor. So does `exp(g) r` with a polynomial `g` in x: `exp(g)` times the series of `r`; the expansions of `erfi` and `dawson_f` keep the constant `-i` and the factor `exp(-x^2)` of their sector (DLMF 7.12.1). Logarithms at `inf` are written as `log(x)`, `log(-x)` or `log(1/x)` (equal for `x > 0`), whichever agrees with the function at the most points off the positive axis (the negative axis, each side of it, the imaginary axis), the shortest among equals; `log(1/x)` stays when the input has symbolic parameters. In the same way an imaginary constant term that the input does not have (it comes from the branch of the function) is written with `i = sqrt(-x^2)/x` (at `-inf`: `-sqrt(-x^2)/x`) when that agrees with the function on more of those points: `atanh(x)` at `inf` starts with `-pi sqrt(-x^2)/(2 x)`, which is `-i pi/2` for `x > 0` and `i pi/2` above the cut and for `x < 0`. The order term and the order of the terms are those of the series in `1/x`: a polynomial of degree d at `inf` is its own series, written from `x^d` down, with the order term `O(x^-(n + 1))`.

#% item
# Sines and cosines of inverse trigonometric functions in the coefficients are written as algebraic functions (`cos(asec(z)) = 1/z`, `sin(asec(z)) = ((z^2 - 1)/z^2)^(1/2)`), identities of the principal values for every complex argument; a coefficient rational in them is first reduced by `cos^2 = 1 - sin^2` when that gives fewer leaves.

#% item
# Gamma functions at rational arguments in the coefficients are written with the fewest leaves (`leaf_count`) among the forms given by `gamma(q + 1) = q gamma(q)` (to the fractional part of q); the computed form stays among equals.

#% item
# At an essential singularity the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The exponential function about 0:

#% cell
series(exp(x), x, 0, 5)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24 + x^5/120 + O(x^6)

#% text
# The sine:

#% cell
series(sin(x), x, 0, 7)
#% output
#= x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 - x^7/5040 + O(x^8)

#% text
# The logarithm about 1:

#% cell
series(log(x), x, 1, 4)
#% output
#= (x - 1) - (x - 1)^2/2 + (x - 1)^3/3 - (x - 1)^4/4 + O((x - 1)^5)

#% text
# The leading term only, with the order of the next term:

#% cell
series(gamma(sin(x) - x)^3, x -> 0)
#% output
#= -216*x^-9 + O(x^-7)

#% text
# A branch point of `asin`, from the side where it is real:

#% cell
series(asin(x), x, 1, 1)
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi - 2^(1/2)*(1 - x)^(1/2) + O((1 - x)^(3/2))

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A removable singularity:

#% cell
series(sin(x)/x, x, 0, 6)
#% output
#= 1 - x^2/6 + x^4/120 - x^6/5040 + O(x^7)

#% text
# A pole gives negative powers:

#% cell
series(1/sin(x), x, 0, 3)
#% output
#= x^-1 + x/6 + 7*x^3/360 + O(x^4)

#% cell
series(1/(x^2 + x), x, 0, 2)
#% output
#= x^-1 - 1 + x - x^2 + O(x^3)

#% text
# Symbolic coefficients:

#% cell
series(a/(1 - b*x), x, 0, 3)
#% output
#= a + a*b*x + a*b^2*x^2 + a*b^3*x^3 + O(x^4)

#% text
# Compositions and products:

#% cell
series(exp(sin(x)), x, 0, 4)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2/2 - x^4/8 + O(x^5)

#% cell
series(tan(x), x, 0, 7)
#% output
#= x + x^3/3 + 2*x^5/15 + 17*x^7/315 + O(x^8)

#% text
# About another point:

#% cell
series(cos(x), x, pi, 3)
#% output
#= -1 + (x - pi)^2/2 + O((x - pi)^4)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Approximate a function near a point by its truncated series, and compare:

#% cell
p = normal(series(sin(x), x, 0, 5)); [p /. x -> 0.1, sin(0.1)]
#% output
#= [0.09983341666666667, 0.09983341664682815]

#% text
# Read off a limit from the leading terms:

#% cell
series((1 - cos(x))/x^2, x, 0, 2)
#% output
#= 1/2 - x^2/24 + O(x^3)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `normal` gives the truncated sum:

#% cell
normal(series(exp(x), x, 0, 3))
#% output
#= 1 + x + (1/2)*x^2 + (1/6)*x^3

#% text
# An order term in the input limits the order of the result:

#% cell
series(cos(x) + O(x^4), x, 0, 6)
#% output
#= 1 - x^2/2 + O(x^4)

#% text
# A polynomial is its own series:

#% cell
series(x^2 + 3x + 1, x, 0, 5)
#% output
#= 1 + 3*x + x^2 + O(x^6)

#% text
# Logarithms at the point and fractional powers give logarithmic and Puiseux series:

#% cell
series(log(x), x, 0, 2)
#% output
#= log(x) + O(x^3)

#% cell
series(cos(sqrt(x)), x, 0, 3)
#% output
#= 1 - x/2 + x^2/24 - x^3/720 + O(x^4)

#% text
# At infinity the logarithm is written in the branch of the function: `log(x)` for `acosh`, `log(-x)` for a hypergeometric function cut along the positive axis:

#% cell
series(acosh(x), x, inf, 3)
#% output
#= log(2) + log(x) - 1/(4*x^2) + O(x^-4)

#% cell
normal(series(hypergeometric_2f1(2, 3, 4, x), x, inf, 5))
#% output
#= -9/x^4 - 6/x^5 + 3/x^2 + (-3 + 6*log(-x))/x^3

#% text
# The constant of `atanh` at infinity with the sign of the side of the cut, and a polynomial at infinity:

#% cell
series(atanh(x), x, inf, 3)
#% output
#= -pi*(-x^2)^(1/2)/(2*x) + x^-1 + 1/(3*x^3) + O(x^-4)

#% cell
series(x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + 1, x, inf, 6)
#% output
#= x^3 + 2*x^2 + 2*x + 1 + O(x^-7)

#% text
# Truncated series combine in arithmetic, to the precision their terms determine:

#% cell
series(sin(x), x, 0, 5)^2 + series(cos(x), x, 0, 5)^2
#% output
#= 1 + O(x^6)

#% cell
1/series(cos(x), x, 0, 4)
#% output
#= 1 + x^2/2 + 5*x^4/24 + O(x^5)

#% text
# `d` differentiates a series term by term:

#% cell
d(series(tan(x), x, 0, 5), x)
#% output
#= 1 + x^2 + 2*x^4/3 + O(x^5)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A substitution into a series keeps the order term; use `normal` first:

#% cell
series(sin(x), x, 0, 3) /. x -> 0.1
#% output
#= 0.1 - 0.1^3/6 + O(0.1^4)

#% text
# When no term of the expansion lies up to the requested order, the series is the order term at the leading power:

#% cell
series(x^(-1/4), x, inf, 0)
#% output
#= O(x^(-1/4))

#% text
# `log(-x)` stays `log(-x)` (valid for either sign of `x`); a symbolic power of the variable moves into the exponents and the order term:

#% cell
series(log(-x), x, 0, 2)
#% output
#= log(-x) + O(x^3)

#% cell
series(x^(m + 1)*exp(x), x, 0, 1)
#% output
#= O(x^(3 + m)) + x^(1 + m) + x^(2 + m)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `normal`, `O`, `limit`, `d`, `coefficient`
